\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces When splitting the register pays. Static costs on the common ledger in Eq.~\eqref  {eq:num-toy-conflict}, evaluated over 181 target separations. Fixed balance costs \(A/4\), optimised arbitration costs \(\min (A/4,0.4)\), and split-enabled allocation costs \(\min (A/4,0.4,0.25)\). All three curves coincide below \(0.8411\) radians, with the green result drawn on top. Above that crossing the green plateau is exactly the grey split-price reference, not a separate lower curve. Arbitration remains equal to fixed balance until \(1.0853\) radians and then caps at \(0.4\). The angle measures target separation. These are calculated static option boundaries, not measured dynamical or biological transitions.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:num-toy-shared-register}{{1}{3}{When splitting the register pays. Static costs on the common ledger in Eq.~\eqref {eq:num-toy-conflict}, evaluated over 181 target separations. Fixed balance costs \(A/4\), optimised arbitration costs \(\min (A/4,0.4)\), and split-enabled allocation costs \(\min (A/4,0.4,0.25)\). All three curves coincide below \(0.8411\) radians, with the green result drawn on top. Above that crossing the green plateau is exactly the grey split-price reference, not a separate lower curve. Arbitration remains equal to fixed balance until \(1.0853\) radians and then caps at \(0.4\). The angle measures target separation. These are calculated static option boundaries, not measured dynamical or biological transitions}{figure.1}{}}
